## Building a Tiny In-Memory Database

A database has **tables**, each table has **rows**, and each row is a set of **column-value pairs**. You can insert, query, update, and delete rows.

### The Core Types

```xiom
type Row = Map[Str, Str];

type Table = {
  name: Str;
  rows: Vec[Row];
}

type QueryResult = {
  matched: Vec[Row];
  count: Int;
}
```

A `Row` is a Map from column names to values. A `Table` holds a Vec of rows. Simple and powerful.

### Creating Tables and Inserting Rows

```xiom
fn Table.new(name: Str) -> Table {
  return Table{ name: name, rows: Vec[Row].new() };
}

fn Table.insert(t: &mut Table, row: Row) {
  t.rows.push(row);
}
```

### Querying by Column

```xiom
fn Table.find_by(t: &Table, column: Str, value: Str) -> QueryResult {
  let matched: Vec[Row] = Vec[Row].new();
  let count = 0;
  let i = 0;
  while i < t.rows.len() {
    let row = match t.rows.get(i) { Some(r) => r, None => continue };
    let cell = match row.get(&column) { Some(v) => v, None => "" };
    if cell == value {
      matched.push(row);
      count = count + 1;
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return QueryResult{ matched: matched, count: count };
}
```

### Deleting Rows

```xiom
fn Table.delete_by(t: &mut Table, column: Str, value: Str) -> Int {
  let kept: Vec[Row] = Vec[Row].new();
  let deleted = 0;
  let i = 0;
  while i < t.rows.len() {
    let row = match t.rows.get(i) { Some(r) => r, None => continue };
    let cell = match row.get(&column) { Some(v) => v, None => "" };
    if cell == value {
      deleted = deleted + 1;
    } else {
      kept.push(row);
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  // Replace rows with filtered list
  while t.rows.len() > 0 {
    t.rows.pop();
  }
  i = 0;
  while i < kept.len() {
    let row = match kept.get(i) { Some(r) => r, None => continue };
    t.rows.push(row);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return deleted;
}
```

### Listing All Rows

```xiom
fn Table.list_all(t: &Table) -> Vec[Row] {
  let result: Vec[Row] = Vec[Row].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < t.rows.len() {
    let row = match t.rows.get(i) { Some(r) => r, None => continue };
    result.push(row);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Using the Database

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var users: Table = Table.new("users");

  // Insert rows
  let row1: Row = Map[Str, Str].new();
  row1.insert("name", "Alice");
  row1.insert("age", "25");
  row1.insert("city", "NYC");
  users.insert(&mut users, row1);

  let row2: Row = Map[Str, Str].new();
  row2.insert("name", "Bob");
  row2.insert("age", "30");
  row2.insert("city", "LA");
  users.insert(&mut users, row2);

  let row3: Row = Map[Str, Str].new();
  row3.insert("name", "Carol");
  row3.insert("age", "25");
  row3.insert("city", "NYC");
  users.insert(&mut users, row3);

  // Query by city
  let in_nyc = users.find_by(&users, "city", "NYC");
  io.println("People in NYC: " + string.int_to_str(in_nyc.count));

  // Delete Alice
  let removed = users.delete_by(&mut users, "name", "Alice");
  io.println("Removed: " + string.int_to_str(removed));
  io.println("Remaining: " + string.int_to_str(users.rows.len()));

  return 0;
}
```

### What You Built

You just implemented: CREATE (insert), READ (find_by, list_all), UPDATE (delete + insert new), DELETE (delete_by). These are the four CRUD operations every database in the world supports. Your mini database may be small, but the concepts are production-grade.

